   {alias:"body",culture:null,segment:null,value:{markup:"<p>Most AI conversations stop at advice: here is the API call, here is the JSON, here is what you should click. Model Context Protocol closes that last mile. It gives an assistant a discoverable set of tools and a consistent way to call them.</p><h2>Work happens where the truth lives</h2><p>In a CMS, the truth is the schema, the media library, and the content tree. Through MCP, an assistant can inspect those structures before acting. It can reuse an existing data type, put media in the right folder, publish a document, and read it back to verify the result.</p><p>That feedback loop is what makes MCP feel different. The assistant is no longer guessing from a screenshot or handing over a checklist. It can collaborate inside the same boundaries as the editor, while every operation remains explicit and reviewable.</p><h2>Capability without losing control</h2><p>MCP tools are narrow contracts rather than unlimited access. A server can expose exactly the operations a team is comfortable automating. The result is a useful balance: real agency, clear guardrails, and an audit trail made of concrete tool calls.</p>",blocks:{layout:{},contentData:[],settingsData:[],expose:[]}}}
