    {alias:"body",value:{markup:"<p>Integrations are usually a collection of private assumptions: an endpoint hidden in a wiki, a script only one person understands, or a plugin tied to one interface. Model Context Protocol turns those assumptions into a shared contract that clients can discover.</p><h2>One connection, many useful tools</h2><p>An MCP server can describe the actions it supports and the shapes those actions accept. A client does not need bespoke UI code for every capability. It can learn that Umbraco offers document types, templates, content, domains, and media operations, then choose the precise tool needed for the job.</p><p>That makes integrations feel native. The same assistant can move from a repository to a CMS without pretending they are the same system, because each server provides the vocabulary and guardrails for its own domain.</p><h2>Portable by design</h2><p>The protocol also separates capability from a particular model or chat product. Teams can invest in a well-designed server and reuse it across compatible clients. Better still, local servers keep development workflows close to the project, so an experiment can be powerful without sending control of the system elsewhere.</p><p>MCP is awesome because it makes interoperability practical: explicit tools, discoverable context, and fewer brittle bridges between people, models, and software.</p>",blocks:{layout:{},contentData:[],settingsData:[],expose:[]}}}
